    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity`.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe rule: **`POST /api/owners` is rejected with 409 when the owner's city already holds 50+ owners.**\n\nThe repository already exposed `countByCity` (used by `CustomerCodeGenerator`), so I built on the existing plumbing rather than duplicating it, and followed the codebase's established pattern for the other conflict rules (`DuplicateTelephoneException`, `DuplicateHouseholdException`):\n\n1. **`ClinicService` / `ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 added `long countOwnersByCity(String city)`, a thin read-only facade over `ownerRepository.countByCity`, keeping controllers off the repository directly like the other owner queries.\n\n2. **`CityCapacityExceededException`** (new, in the `rest.validation` package) \u2014 a small `RuntimeException` carrying the offending city, mirroring the existing duplicate exceptions.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a `MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY = 50` constant and a guard that throws `CityCapacityExceededException` when `countOwnersByCity(city) >= 50`, placed alongside the other pre-save conflict checks.\n\n4. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added a handler mapping the new exception to **409 Conflict** with a `ProblemDetail` and `errors: [\"city\"]`, consistent with the other 409 handlers.\n\nEach piece sits in its natural layer (service query, domain exception, controller rule, exception-to-HTTP mapping), and no class accumulated unrelated logic.",
